# Display Engine Rules (MANDATORY)

The render loop runs on the process's main OS thread, pinned with `runtime.LockOSThread()` whenever display output is enabled. On headless Linux that thread typically holds DRM master. These rules are not stylistic.

## Thread Ownership

1. **SDL is called only from the render thread.** No exceptions — not from an HTTP handler, not from a decode worker, not "just to grab a screenshot". Anything that needs display data reads the published state snapshot instead.
2. **Texture uploads are render-thread only.** `SDL_UpdateNVTexture` and friends cannot be moved off it.
3. **Every decode worker calls `runtime.LockOSThread()`** and unlocks on exit. libav calls are cgo and long-running; leaving them on scheduler threads produces erratic latency across the whole process.

## The Loop Never Blocks

4. **No blocking operations in the render loop.** No database, no network, no filesystem, no subprocess, no channel receive without a `default`, no mutex that another plane can hold.
5. **The engine never reads the database.** The control plane resolves configuration into an immutable `strategy.Snapshot` and sends it over the command channel.
6. **Bound every per-frame operation.** Command drains, scene size, and texture allocation all need ceilings so one frame cannot stretch indefinitely.
7. **Pump the SDL event queue every iteration.** Under KMSDRM, failing to do so produces a black screen with no error.

## State Exchange

8. **Commands carry immutable values**, never a pointer to state the sender will keep mutating.
9. **Published state is replaced, not mutated.** Build a new struct and `Store` it through the `atomic.Pointer`.
10. **Frame slots queue a bounded handful of frames and evict the oldest when full.** The bound is what stops the renderer falling behind permanently; the queue is what lets it schedule presentation, which a newest-only handoff cannot express. Never make the queue unbounded, never block the producer on it, and never hold its mutex across a frame copy.
10a. **Present by media time, not by arrival.** Each source has a presentation clock anchoring its PTS to the wall clock; the renderer picks the frame due at the *next* refresh. Sampling "whatever is newest" makes cadence depend on the phase between two unrelated clocks, which judders on moving content and hides on a fixed camera.
10b. **Take the refresh interval from the display mode, never from the loop period.** Timing ourselves folds our own overruns into the estimate, so a loop that falls behind concludes the panel slowed down and schedules against a refresh that does not exist. Loop timing is a fallback for drivers that report no mode.
10c. **Never synchronise sources to each other.** They have no common timebase; each keeps its own clock and the display is master only for presentation. Do spread their *anchors* so a grid does not converge its uploads onto one refresh — one overrun hitches every tile at once, which is worse than any single stream's timing.
11. **Unbind before teardown.** The engine releases a slot before the worker owning it is cancelled, so the renderer can never sample buffers being freed.

## Correctness

12. **Animation progress comes from the wall clock, never a frame counter.** A frame-counted transition changes speed exactly when frames drop, which is when it is most visible.
13. **Round tile geometry to whole pixels.** Sub-pixel edges shimmer on a static wall.
13a. **All texture uploads for a pass precede all draw calls in that pass**, so no tile is ever presented half-old and half-new. Check that `SDL_SetRenderVSync` succeeded and report failure; without vsync the wall tears and nothing else will say why.
14. **Set the texture colorspace explicitly.** YUV textures default to `SDL_COLORSPACE_JPEG` (full-range BT.601); most real video is limited-range BT.709. Use `SDL_CreateTextureWithProperties` with `SDL_PROP_TEXTURE_CREATE_COLORSPACE_NUMBER`.
15. **Every libav allocation has a matching unref**, established at the allocation site with `defer`. Leaked `AVFrame`/`AVPacket` references are invisible until the process is out of memory.

## Degrade, Never Stall

16. A missing frame holds the last one; a failed texture shows a placeholder; an unavailable shader falls back to `fade`. **Nothing stops the loop.** Record every degradation in the state snapshot so the UI can surface it.

